<TopicPage slug="subjects/hospitality-tourism/foundational/global-tourism">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    International tourism is one of the world's largest movements of people and money: in 2019
    about <strong>1.46 billion international tourist arrivals</strong> were recorded worldwide —
    and in 2020, about 407 million. Cambridge IGCSE Travel &amp; Tourism 0471 devotes topic 2,
    "Global tourism", to that story: the <strong>scale of travel and tourism</strong> (2.1), the
    forces that drive demand, and the <strong>features of destinations and their appeal to
    different types of tourists</strong> (2.2). Behind the numbers sit the topic 1 definitions
    you need first: domestic, inbound, outbound and international tourism; leisure, business
    (MICE), visiting friends and relatives, and special-interest travel.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="international tourist arrivals worldwide, 1995–2023 (UNWTO) — thirty years of growth, one cliff, one rebound" widget="plotter" config={arrivalsCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the curve the way the syllabus asks you to read demand. From 1995 to 2019, arrivals
    more than doubled — a long, steady climb powered by rising disposable income and cheaper
    transport. Then 2020: a fall of roughly 72% in a single year when the health factor
    (syllabus 2.1a: "risk of disease, pandemics") overrode everything else. The rebound to
    about 1.29 billion by 2023 shows the resilience the syllabus lists among the
    <em>characteristics of travel and tourism</em> — a product that is perishable (an empty
    2020 hotel room is gone forever) but a demand that survives shocks.
  </p>

  <h2>What moves demand</h2>
  <p>
    Syllabus 2.1(a) sorts the drivers into six families, and every one is visible in the chart:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Economic</strong> — levels of disposable income and government investment; the long climb tracks a world getting richer.</li>
    <li><strong>Social</strong> — demographics, fashion and trends; the beach holiday went from novelty to expectation within two generations.</li>
    <li><strong>Technological</strong> — developments in transport and information technology: jets, jumbos, budget fares, online booking and social media.</li>
    <li><strong>Political</strong> — stability, security, terrorism and imposed limits on travel; borders closing or reopening move the line instantly.</li>
    <li><strong>Environmental</strong> — extreme weather, natural disasters, and growing concern for the environment feeding the growth of sustainable tourism.</li>
    <li><strong>Health</strong> — pandemics above all; 2020 is the case study.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A driver worth watching separately is the growth of <strong>sustainable tourism</strong>
    (2.1b). Changing customer attitudes, media and social media influence, the wider
    availability and promotion of sustainable products, and government policies are pushing
    demand towards travel that protects destinations rather than consuming them — which matters
    because tourism, left unmanaged, can destroy the very features that made a destination
    appealing. The same demand that fills a cove can erode it.
  </p>

  <h2>A century and a half of cheaper distance</h2>
  <p>
    The growth curve did not happen on its own — each step change came from a transport or
    business innovation that cut the cost of distance.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="from a shilling rail ticket to the jumbo jet to the budget airline — how mass travel was engineered" widget="timeline" config={tourismTimeline} />

  <h2>What makes a destination appeal</h2>
  <p>
    Syllabus 2.2 turns from demand to supply: why this place and not that one? The listed
    features of destinations are a checklist of appeal — <strong>location; weather and seasonal
    characteristics; topographical features; accessibility; travel and transport gateways and
    hubs; infrastructure</strong> such as roads, airports and ports; <strong>built and natural
    attractions; culture</strong> (traditions, language, arts, music); expected behaviours;
    tourism organisations and providers; and sustainable provisions. The analytical skill is
    matching features to tourist types: families want safe beaches and short transfers;
    ecotourists want unspoiled topography and responsible operators; the business traveller
    (MICE) wants gateways, hubs and conference infrastructure and cares little for the view. A
    destination is not simply "good" — it is good <em>for</em> someone, and managing it well
    (2.3) means maximising the positive impacts of tourism while minimising the negative ones.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the appeal list is a moving target. A destination's features are read through the type
    of tourism in question — domestic, inbound, outbound, international, a day trip or a short
    break (syllabus 1.1 and 1.6 classify both the trips and the destinations: beach and coastal,
    island, resort, rural, urban) — and through what each tourist expects and can afford.
    Accessibility can be transformed within a decade by a new airport route; a culture can become
    an attraction because a film or a feed made it visible. When you analyse a destination in
    the exam, the marks come from linking a named feature to a named tourist type, and then to
    how the feature is being managed or sustained — scale, appeal and management in one chain of
    reasoning.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Travel &amp; Tourism 0471 syllabus for 2027–2029, topics 1–2 ("Global tourism": 2.1 The scale of travel and tourism; 2.2 Features of destinations); UNWTO/UN Tourism, <em>World Tourism Barometer</em> (2024) and <em>Tourism Highlights</em>; Thomas Cook archive.</p>
</TopicPage>
